# `{{ distribution }}` wheelhouse

Every wheel this scenario needs, resolved once when the package was exported.
Installing from it needs **pip and nothing else** -- no `uv`, no `git`, no
network, and no resolution:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
.venv/{{ venv_bin }}/pip install --no-index --find-links . {{ distribution }}
```

To install exactly the versions that were resolved, rather than letting pip
pick the best match in the directory:

```bash
.venv/{{ venv_bin }}/pip install --no-index --find-links . -r requirements.txt
```

Nothing has to be built afterwards. The CARLA client is typesafe_carla
(`import typesafe_carla.carla as carla`), and the `typesafe-carla` wheel in
here carries its CPython package prebuilt, for every Python 3.10+, so the
install above is the whole of it: no C compiler, no build step.

Only if that prebuilt package does not match the installation -- a
`typesafe-carla-toolchain` other than the one the wheel was built with -- does
the first `import typesafe_carla.carla`, the first scenario run, build it with
the Codon compiler from the toolchain wheel: 15 to 50 minutes, about 14 GB of
RAM and a C compiler (`cc`), but no network. In that case
`.venv/{{ venv_bin }}/typesafe-codon pycarla` does it ahead of time (and
reports that there is nothing to build when the prebuilt package applies); the
build lands in `~/.cache/typesafe_carla/pycarla`, or in
`TYPESAFE_CARLA_PYCARLA_DIR` if set.

Then run the scenario:

```bash
.venv/{{ venv_bin }}/{{ run_command }}
```

## What is in here

| | |
| --- | --- |
| Distribution | `{{ distribution }}=={{ version }}` |
| Wheels | {{ wheel_count }} |
| Python | {{ pythons | join(", ") }} |

`manifest.yaml` records what this was built from -- the framework's commit, the
interpreters, the uv version, and any caveat the export raised.

The scenario document and its Hydra config travel **inside** the scenario's own
wheel, so the installed package is the scenario -- there is no directory that
has to be kept next to it.

## What it is not

A wheelhouse is resolved for **one platform**. These wheels were selected on the
machine that exported the package, so installing them on a different platform
fails on the first wheel that has no matching tag -- re-export on a matching
machine instead.

The interpreter is the one axis it covers more than one of: the directory was
resolved once per Python in the table above{% if pythons | length > 1 %}, so the compiled extensions -- OpenCV
and the lanelet2 bindings among them -- are in here once per interpreter and
pip takes the tag it can install{% endif %}. A Python outside that list has no wheels here.
